Two nx_secure record files, copied and edited for RFC 7905
==========================================================

READ THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE
------------------------------

**These two files SHADOW the vendored ones.  A security fix that lands in
`third_party/netxduo/nx_secure/src/nx_secure_tls_record_payload_*.c` does NOT
reach the binary until it is ported here by hand.**  The build excludes the
vendored pair from the glob, so the submodule can be bumped, the fix can be
sitting in the tree, `git log` in the submodule can show it, and the compiled
record path is still the old code.  Nothing warns.

It has already happened once.  Submodule commit `1b9fb637`, "nx_secure: give
each record path its own block buffer", replaced a file-scope `UCHAR[128]`
shared by five functions across both record paths.  Three uses held data in it
across `nx_packet_data_append(..., NX_WAIT_FOREVER)`, which suspends when the
packet pool is empty -- which is exactly when a second session is running, so
one session resumed into another's bytes: plaintext copied into the wrong
connection's packet on the decrypt side, corrupted CBC padding on the encrypt
side.  The submodule was fixed and the shipped binary was not, because these
copies still had the shared buffer and nothing here said they had to track it.

So, after ANY NetX Duo bump, run the `diff -u` under "The whole of the diff"
below and account for every line it prints.  The expected output is fixed and
short; anything beyond it is an upstream change that has to be ported or
consciously rejected, and a security fix is not something to reject quietly.
`tools/check-shadowed-sources.sh` -- the `shadowed_sources` ctest -- is the
automated form of the same check, and it covers the other shadowed file in the
tree, `nx_ip_checksum_compute.c`, for the same reason.

`nx_secure_tls_record_payload_encrypt.c` and
`nx_secure_tls_record_payload_decrypt.c` here are copies of the files of the
same name in `third_party/netxduo/nx_secure/src/`, with one branch added to
each.  `src/tls/CMakeLists.txt` drops the vendored pair from the glob so that
these are the ones every internal caller resolves to -- the same mechanism the
top level uses for `_nx_ip_checksum_compute()`, and for the same reason: a
NetX Duo inner loop can be replaced without editing `third_party/`.

Why a copy and not a hook
-------------------------

`nx_secure` has exactly one extension point here, `NX_SECURE_AEAD_CIPHER_CHECK`,
and it is the wrong shape.  It widens the *test* for "is this an AEAD cipher",
and then `nx_secure_tls_session_iv_size_get.c` converts anything it matches to
`NX_CRYPTO_ENCRYPTION_AES_GCM_16` and the two files here build a GCM nonce.
That is the TLS 1.2 partially-explicit construction: a four-byte implicit salt,
an eight-byte nonce_explicit written at the head of every record, and the
sequence number as the nonce's low half.

RFC 7905 does none of that.  ChaCha20-Poly1305 in TLS 1.2 uses the construction
TLS 1.3 later adopted for everything: `record_iv_length` is **zero**, nothing
precedes the ciphertext, and the per-record nonce is the full twelve-byte write
IV exclusive-ored with the sequence number padded on the left.  Only the
additional data stays TLS 1.2's thirteen bytes.  So the difference is on the
wire, in the record's length and layout, and no amount of work inside an
`NX_CRYPTO_METHOD` can reach it -- a method is handed a nonce and a buffer
after the framing has already been decided.

`nx_secure_tls_session_iv_size_get.c` is deliberately NOT copied.  Its `switch`
has no case for our algorithm, so it takes the `default:` and returns zero,
which is what RFC 7905 wants.  Leaving `NX_SECURE_AEAD_CIPHER_CHECK` at its
default of `NX_FALSE` is what keeps it there.

The whole of the diff
---------------------

Both files carry the RFC 7905 change in the same place: the `#ifdef
NX_SECURE_ENABLE_AEAD_CIPHER` block that decides the nonce and the additional
data.

1. `NX_CRYPTO_ENCRYPTION_CHACHA20_POLY1305` added to the `if` that selects the
   AEAD path.  (The constant is the vendored one, `nx_crypto_const.h`; it was
   already reserved.)

2. A branch ahead of the TLS 1.3 test that builds the RFC 7905 nonce and
   additional data.  In the encrypt file it leaves `*data_offset` at zero and
   takes `message_length` as the whole packet, where the GCM path prepends
   eight bytes and subtracts them; in the decrypt file it leaves `offset`
   alone, where the GCM path extracts eight bytes and advances past them.

The decrypt file has one further difference, and it is not an RFC 7905 one:

3. `save_iv` moved from file scope into `_nx_secure_tls_record_payload_decrypt`.
   Upstream still has it as a `static UCHAR[20]`.  Only its size is ever read --
   it is the bound on `iv_size` before the session IV is filled, and no byte is
   ever stored in it -- so unlike the block buffer it never leaked anything.
   It is the same shape though, mutable state at file scope in a path two
   sessions run at once, and the shape is what went wrong once already.  A local
   costs nothing and is correct the day somebody adds a write.

Nothing else is touched.  `diff -u` against the submodule shows only those
hunks and the file header comment, and that is the check to run after a NetX
Duo bump:

    for f in nx_secure_tls_record_payload_encrypt.c \
             nx_secure_tls_record_payload_decrypt.c; do
        diff -u third_party/netxduo/nx_secure/src/$f src/tls/rfc7905/$f
    done

`tools/check-shadowed-sources.sh` is the automated half: it pins the hash of
each vendored file its shadow was reconciled against and fails when upstream
moves, so a bump cannot quietly leave the diff above out of date.  It runs as
the `shadowed_sources` ctest in every host build.  Update the hash in the same
commit that ports the upstream change.

`tests/tls/tls_https` and the curl suite are what say the merge was done right;
the RFC 8439 vectors in `tests/crypto68k` only say the arithmetic is.

